Description: nouveau: give NV12/P010 a 64-byte stride
 On an Intel iGPU + NVIDIA dGPU laptop, arc-setup 010 renders Android on NVK,
 so gralloc allocates video decoder output through this backend, but Chrome's
 VA-API decoder on the Intel iGPU fills those buffers. It writes NV12 at a pitch
 of ALIGN(width, 64) whatever stride it is handed, so with the 32-byte
 nouveau_stride() any video whose width is not a multiple of 64 came out sheared
 (red/green diagonal stripes) and the decoder wrote past the end of the buffer.
 Seen on device (i5-12450H + RTX 2050): Netflix's 864x288 intro was written at
 pitch 896 into a buffer laid out at 864; decode tests at 864/832/800 wide all
 wrote ALIGN(width, 64). 64 is still a whole number of NVK's 32-byte pitch
 units, so NVK imports are unaffected.
Author: Yang Tsao <yang@fydeos.io>

--- a/nouveau.c
+++ b/nouveau.c
@@ -249,6 +249,16 @@
 	 */
 	if (format == DRM_FORMAT_YVU420 || format == DRM_FORMAT_YVU420_ANDROID)
 		align = 64;
+	/*
+	 * NV12/P010 are video decoder output on a dual-GPU laptop, where Chrome's
+	 * VA-API decoder on the Intel iGPU writes into these buffers. It writes at a
+	 * pitch of ALIGN(width, 64) whatever stride it is handed, so a 32-only
+	 * stride left every frame whose width is not a multiple of 64 sheared, and
+	 * overran the buffer (Netflix's 864x288 intro on device: written at pitch
+	 * 896 into a buffer laid out at 864).
+	 */
+	if (format == DRM_FORMAT_NV12 || format == DRM_FORMAT_P010)
+		align = 64;
 	while (cpp && align % cpp)
 		align += 32;
 
